<section id="takeaways">
  <h2>Takeaways</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>We present a general-purpose method for generating dense 3D segmentations from sparse 2D annotations. It works across diverse imaging modalities, biological targets, and segmentation tasks. No domain-specific pretraining or high-performance computing is required.</li>
    <li>Specialist 3D models trained on dense ground-truth achieve the highest accuracy within their domain. This method addresses the bottleneck that precedes them: generating the dense training data needed for both model development and evaluation.</li>
    <li>In our experiments, bootstrapped 3D models trained on unproofread pseudo ground-truth approached the quality of models trained on dense expert annotations, with orders-of-magnitude less human effort.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Foundation models</h3>

  <p>The pipeline is complementary to segmentation foundation models. SAM, microSAM, or CellSAM outputs serve directly as sparse input labels for the 2D&rarr;3D framework. As 2D foundation models improve, so do the segmentations bootstrapped through them, with no changes to the pipeline.</p>

  <h3>Toward autonomous bootstrapping</h3>

  <p>The bootstrapper CLI is fully scriptable. AI agents can independently run the entire bootstrapping loop: selecting annotations, training models, running inference, monitoring LSD and segmentation inconsistencies, and iterating.</p>

  <p>Dense voxel ground-truth remains the gold standard for 3D microscopy segmentation. This method reduces the total cost of generating training data.</p>
</section>
